## 📋 Steps I Followed

**Step 1 — Loaded Dataset**
Imported HR Employee Attrition dataset into Power BI
Desktop using Get Data → Excel/CSV. Dataset had 1,470+
employee records across 35 columns.

**Step 2 — Cleaned Data in Power Query**
Opened Transform Data → Power Query Editor.
Checked nulls, fixed data types, cleaned categorical
columns (Gender, Department, JobRole, Attrition).
Clicked Close & Apply.

**Step 3 — Created DAX Measures**
Wrote 4 custom DAX measures:
- Count of Employees
- Average Age
- Average Monthly Income = AVERAGE(MonthlyIncome)
- Avg Working Years = AVERAGE(TotalWorkingYears)
- Salary Hike % = AVERAGE(PercentSalaryHike)
- Sum of Hourly Rate = SUM(HourlyRate)

**Step 4 — Built 6 KPI Cards**
Added Card visuals for each DAX measure.
Placed all KPI cards in top row of dashboard.

**Step 5 — Built Donut Chart (Employees by Overtime)**
Added Donut Chart → dragged OverTime to Legend,
Employee ID to Values. Shows Yes/No overtime split.

**Step 6 — Built Pie Chart (Employees by Gender)**
Added Pie Chart → dragged Gender to Legend,
Employee ID count to Values.
Result: Male 69.77% | Female 30.23%

**Step 7 — Built Line Chart (Income by Job Role)**
Added Line Chart → JobRole on X-axis,
Monthly Income and Employee ID count on Y-axis.
Used dual line to compare headcount vs income.

**Step 8 — Built Table (Employee Detail)**
Added Table visual → columns: Employee ID, JobRole,
Gender, Sum of MonthlyIncome.
Shows total income of ₹2,81,395 across all roles.

**Step 9 — Built Bar Chart (Education Field Analysis)**
Added Horizontal Bar Chart → EducationField on Y-axis,
Count of Employee ID + Count of Education on X-axis.
Life Sciences topped the chart.

**Step 10 — Added 7 Slicers**
Added slicers for: Department, Gender, HourlyRate (slider),
PerformanceRating (slider), JobLevel, JobSatisfaction,
Attrition (Yes/No). All charts update on every slicer click.

**Step 11 — Formatted the Dashboard**
Applied pink/purple colour theme throughout.
Added borders and backgrounds to all visuals.
Arranged layout: KPI cards top, slicers left,
charts in centre and right.

**Step 12 — Saved & Published**
Saved as .pbix → took screenshot → posted on LinkedIn
→ got 9,254+ impressions and 100+ likes 🔥
